course: english
lesson: 47
title: Poetry forms: haiku, limerick, kenning, acrostic, performing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A poem's form is its shape and its rules, and many forms are like games with one rule to keep.
explain: A haiku (from Japan) is, in English schools, three lines of 5, 7 and 5 syllables catching one small moment; a limerick is a funny five-line poem whose lines 1, 2 and 5 rhyme, and lines 3 and 4 rhyme with each other (AABBA).
explain: A kenning names a thing with two-word phrases, often a noun and an "-er" word ("ball-kicker", "goal-scorer"), an old Viking and Anglo-Saxon trick; an acrostic spells a word down the first letters of its lines; a shape poem is laid out in the shape of its subject.
explain: Free verse has no regular rhyme or beat, but the poet breaks the lines where the reader should pause, so it is still poetry, not chopped-up prose.
explain: Picture Ruby counting a haiku on her fingers, "Au-tumn leaves fall-ing" (five beats), and Liam in Leeds groaning "Performin' poems is well scary, Miss" ("well" means very): reading aloud slowly and pausing at line ends brings any form to life.
fact: In English schools a haiku is usually three lines of 5, 7 and 5 syllables; the form comes from Japan.
fact: A syllable is one beat in a word: "au-tumn" has two beats, "fall-ing" has two, "leaves" has one.
fact: Haiku are often about nature, but they can catch any small everyday moment, even at a petrol station.
fact: "Servo" is Australian for a petrol station.
fact: A limerick is a funny five-line poem with the rhyme pattern AABBA and a bouncy rhythm.
fact: Edward Lear (1812–1888) made the limerick famous; his "There was an Old Man with a beard" is public domain.
fact: A kenning describes something with two-word phrases, such as "ball-kicker" for a footballer; kennings do not need to rhyme.
fact: An acrostic poem spells a word with the first letter of each line, and each line should be a phrase, not just one word.
fact: A shape poem (also called a calligram) is laid out in the shape of its subject, but its words must still be strong.
fact: Free verse has no regular rhyme or rhythm and uses line breaks for effect.
fact: Performing a poem with clear speech, pauses at line ends and actions helps listeners feel it and helps you remember it; you do not need to be an actor.
fact: "Bussin'" is Gen Alpha slang for great, and "well" in Leeds and much of England can mean "very".
exercise: How many syllables are in the three lines of a school haiku? | 5, then 7, then 5 | 7, then 5, then 7 | 3, then 5, then 3 | 5, then 5, then 5
exercise: Ruby counts "Au-tumn leaves fall-ing" on her fingers. How many syllables is that? | 5 | 4 | 3 | 6
exercise: Where does the haiku come from? | Japan | China | Ancient Greece | Wales
exercise: Which line has exactly 7 syllables? | The cat sleeps on the warm wall | The cat sleeps on the wall | The big cat sleeps on the warm wall | Cats sleep on warm walls
exercise: What is the rhyme pattern of a limerick? | AABBA | ABABA | AAAAA | ABCDE
exercise: Who made the limerick famous with "There was an Old Man with a beard"? | Edward Lear | William Shakespeare | Roald Dahl | Lewis Carroll
exercise: Kai writes, "Ball-kicker, Goal-scorer, Crowd-pleaser." Which form is it? | A kenning | A haiku | A limerick | An acrostic
exercise: Which is a kenning for a cat? | Mouse-catcher | Furry and soft | Like a small tiger | Cats are pets
exercise: Do kennings have to rhyme? | No, they are two-word naming phrases | Yes, each one must rhyme with the next | Yes, but only the last two need to rhyme | No, because a kenning is a kind of limerick
exercise: In an acrostic for SNOW, what starts each line? | S, N, O and W, in that order | The word SNOW, again and again | A word that rhymes with snow | Any letter, as long as it is cold
exercise: Which is the better acrostic line for the S of SNOW? | Soft flakes falling on the hill | S, and nothing else after it | Snow | Sss
exercise: The teacher wants a shape poem about a snake. What should it look like? | Lines laid out as a long wavy snake | A plain block of lines with a snake title | Three lines of 5, 7 and 5 syllables only | Five lines rhyming AABBA about a snake
exercise: Year 6 reads a poem with no rhyme. "Free verse," says the teacher. What is free verse? | Poetry with no regular rhyme or beat | Prose cut up into lines at random | Any poem you do not pay to read | A poem that is only one line long
exercise: Liam says, "Performin' poems is well scary, Miss." What helps most? | Speak clearly and pause at line ends | Read as fast as possible to get it done | Look at the floor so nobody sees you | Only perform if you are a trained actor
exercise: A mum in Sydney asks for "a haiku about the servo". What is a servo? | A petrol station | A school bus | A beach shop | A swimming pool
exercise: A Gen Alpha kid says, "Limericks are bussin', can I do another?" What does he mean? | Limericks are great | Limericks are boring | Limericks are too long | Limericks are confusing
test: Your mate says, "Any short poem's a haiku, innit." Put him right. | A haiku keeps a 5-7-5 syllable pattern | Any poem under ten words is a haiku | A haiku is any poem that rhymes twice | A haiku must be about the sea, only that
test: Ella writes the line "Petrol smell, cars hum". Can it open a haiku? | Yes, it has five syllables | No, it has four syllables | No, it has seven syllables | No, haiku can't be about cars
test: Grandad, dry: "Back in my day poems rhymed, end of." The teacher shows free verse. What is true? | Free verse is real poetry built on line breaks | No rhyme means no poem, so Grandad is right | It is prose with lines cut at random places | Free verse is a modern name for a limerick
test: What would happen to a limerick if line 5 did not rhyme with line 1? | It would break the AABBA pattern | It would turn into a haiku | Nothing; line 5 is free | It would turn into a kenning
test: A cartoon says a kenning is "a poem about a bloke called Ken". Fix it. | It names a thing in two-word phrases | It is any poem written by a Viking | It is a poem of exactly ten lines | The cartoon is right about Ken
test: Shona in Aberdeen: "It's dreich the day, I'll dae a kenning for the rain." ("Dreich" means grey and gloomy; "the day" means today.) Which is a kenning for rain? | Puddle-maker | Wet and grey all day | As cold as tears | Rain falls down
test: Which poem is an acrostic for CAT? | Curled up / Asleep in the sun / Tail twitching | My cat is cool / And very fun / She is grey | A cat sat / on a mat / and that is that | Cat, cat, cat / cat, cat, cat / cat, cat, cat
test: Jordan from Bristol: "I done a shape poem, proper job, it's a wave but there's only one word on it." ("Proper job" means a job well done.) What is missing? | Strong words, since the words matter too | Nothing; one word is the perfect shape poem | An AABBA rhyme pattern running through it | A title of 5, 7 and 5 syllables on top
test: Year 1 performs "Twinkle, twinkle" with actions. Why do actions help? | They help memory and show meaning | They let the class rush through it | They mean you can skip the words | They turn the rhyme into a limerick
test: Which list gives a haiku, a limerick and a kenning, in that order? | 5-7-5 lines; funny AABBA lines; "goal-scorer" | funny AABBA lines; 5-7-5 lines; "goal-scorer" | "goal-scorer"; 5-7-5 lines; funny AABBA lines | 5-7-5 lines; "goal-scorer"; funny AABBA lines
test: Can "The old dog sleeps by the door" be the first line of a haiku? | No: it has 7 syllables, not 5 | Yes: it has exactly 5 syllables | No: it has 9, far too many | Yes: line one can be any length
exam: Identify the poetic form, of Japanese origin, customarily composed in English schools as three lines of five, seven and five syllables respectively. | The haiku | The limerick | The kenning | The acrostic
exam: State the rhyme scheme conventionally observed in the limerick. | AABBA | ABABA | AABB | ABCB
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises a kenning. | A two-word phrase that names a thing | A poem whose first letters spell a word | A poem arranged in the shape of its subject | A humorous verse five lines in length
exam: Determine which of the following is a defining feature of free verse. | The absence of regular rhyme or rhythm | A rhyme at the end of every single line | A length of precisely fourteen lines | An arrangement in the shape of its subject
